        // But is there a case in reference where this fails? Let's double-check: Reference after a loss: balance and pot both increase by 10. After a win: both become 0. So invariant holds. Even if there are multiple wins/losses, they always reset or increment consistently. So yes, universal.
        // So the property: "The contract's ETH balance is always equal to the pot value".
        // We can assert address(c).balance == c.pot() after the fuzzed sequence.
        // The fuzzed parameters: numPlays, seed. We'll loop and alternate wins/losses as before, but now we don't need to force every play to be a win; just ensure that some wins occur (which they will with our alternating pattern).
        // However, we must ensure at least one win occurs. If numPlays=1 and we force loss (warp/roll make random!=0), then the invariant might hold for both (balance==pot). That's fine; the reference passes for all inputs, and the mutant will fail for some input (those where a win occurs). The fuzzer will eventually try numPlays such that a win happens (especially since we alternate). So the domain is wide.
        // But we also need to consider that the fuzzer can call testFuzz_property with any numPlays, seed. We'll use bound to keep numPlays reasonable. The invariant holds for all numPlays in reference, so even if the fuzzer picks a large number, reference passes.

        address(c).balance; // just reading it; not needed
        // Actually, we can set up the sequence without knowing whether the last was a win. The invariant holds regardless.
        // Use vm.deal to fund players and then prank them.
        // Since we need to set block.timestamp and difficulty for each play to control random, we'll warp and roll in each iteration.
        // We'll decide win/loss pattern by i % 2, but that's just our choice. The test will hold for reference no matter what.

        for (uint256 i = 0; i < numPlays; i++) {
            address player = address(uint160(uint256(keccak256(abi.encode(i, seed)))));
            vm.deal(player, 100 ether);
            vm.prank(player);
            // Force random outcome based on i parity to ensure wins and losses both occur.
            if (i % 2 == 0) {
                // win
                vm.warp(1);
                vm.roll(2);
            } else {
                vm.warp(2);
                vm.roll(1);
            }
            c.play{value: 10}();
        }

        // Invariant
        assertTrue(address(c).balance == c.pot());
    }
}

